 2. Consumer checkup 
 
 
 &nbsp; 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 Illustration: Sarah Grillo/Axios

 
 
 
 &nbsp; 
 
 Walmart raised its sales and earnings outlook as its online business continues to surge.

 Why it matters: The retailer is a bellwether for the broader economy, offering a window into the health of the American consumer. 🗣️ What they're saying: "The words we would use to describe the customer are stable, resilient," CEO John Furner said on this morning's earnings call.

 In the U.S., customer transactions — excluding the fuel pump — rose 1.5% in comparable channels in the quarter ended Oct. 31 compared to a year earlier.

 The average size of those tickets increased 1.4%. Market share gains in grocery categories were some of the highest the company has seen in three years. Upper-income households drove share gains across categories. 💻 The intrigue: eCommerce sales grew 17% in the quarter, and now represent 24% of Walmart's sales.

 📊 Zoom out: The retailer posted $188.8 billion revenue for the quarter, up 5.2% from a year earlier and topping S&amp;P Capital IQ expectations of $187.2 billion.

 It also increased its fiscal year guidance for net sales from a previous range of up 4%-5% to a new range of up 4.9%-5.2%. 📉 Yes, but: The rate of comparable sales growth slowed in its Walmart U.S. division.

 U.S. comp sales, which include online sales and sales at stores open at least a year, rose 2.9% in the quarter excluding fuel, down from 4.5% in the same period a year earlier. 💊 The company noted that its pharmacy business took a significant hit from Medicare drug price negotiations. Shares closed down 1% today, but are still up over 25% for the year.

